unit: radioactivity

post blog-nobody-knows-when
author: npc-ada
lesson: why-nuclei-decay
title: nobody knows when
hook: one unstable nucleus is a surprise. a whole crowd of them follows a pattern. isn't that neat?
cover: radiation
read: 1
unlock: {"trigger":"lesson_complete","lessonId":"why-nuclei-decay"}
reactors: npc-hal
references: ref-iaea-decay
references: ref-openstax-up3-ch10

p:
    ok so take one unstable nucleus. leave it alone. it has a chance of changing, and nobody can say when. eep, so mysterious~

p:
    nobody pushes it. the nucleus that changes is the parent. the nucleus it becomes is the daughter. the change gives off radiation

quote:
    a half-life ticks for a crowd of atoms, never for one

h: think of a die

p:
    roll a die once and you can't say if it will show a six. roll it 600 times and you can say that about 100 of the rolls will. hehe

p:
    a nucleus is like that. one is a surprise. a big group follows a pattern, and in the half-life lesson you'll see the pattern for yourself. it's so pretty~

figure: radiation
caption: The radiation sign, used to mark a source of radiation.

fact:
    Heating, cooling and chemistry hardly change how fast an unstable nucleus changes. In a flame or in ice, it is still unstable.

list:
    One nucleus: you can't say when
    A big group: it follows a pattern

p:
    so when someone asks you when an atom will change, you can say: you can't know. a crowd of them, though, you can know. hehe, tiny mystery solved~

next: see what comes out of a decay => lesson alpha-beta-gamma

comment bc-nobody-knows-when-ray
